@c ====================================================================
@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction
@cindex introduction
@cindex Gas City
@cindex porcelain

Gas City is a system for running fleets of coding agents.  Its
@command{gc} command-line tool is the @dfn{plumbing}: it starts and
stops agents, routes work, and stores issues.  @code{gascity.el} is the
@dfn{porcelain}: a set of sectioned, keyboard-driven Emacs buffers that
render @command{gc}'s state and run its commands, in the deliberate style
of Magit and Forge.

The vocabulary is fixed.  Every term the manual leans on --- @dfn{city},
@dfn{rig}, @dfn{agent}, @dfn{session}, @dfn{polecat}, @dfn{bead},
@dfn{convoy}, @dfn{order}, @dfn{sling}, @dfn{formula}, @dfn{workflow},
@dfn{store} and @dfn{live refresh} --- is defined once in @ref{Concepts
and Terminology}, and each chapter links back to @ref{Terminology} the
first time it uses one.

The manual has two reading orders.  The reference chapters document every
view, key and user option in full.  The task-oriented chapters are the
other order: they start from something you want to do --- install the
package, route work to an agent, read the cockpit, drive a city on
another host --- and point at the view and keys that do it.  Read a task
chapter when you are trying to get something done, then follow its
cross-reference to the chapter that documents the view in full.
@xref{Area to Screenshot Map} says which screenshot shows each feature
area.

@menu
* Every view is a function of gc output::  The one rule behind the package.
* What you get::  The feature map.
* Two kinds of buffer::  vui and tabulated-list-mode.
@end menu

@node Every view is a function of gc output
@section Every view is a function of @command{gc} output

@cindex JSON
@cindex data plane
There is one rule behind the whole package: every view is a function of
@code{gc @dots{} --json} output.  A single module runs @command{gc},
decodes its JSON, and hands typed objects to the views; the views render
those objects and bind keys back to @command{gc} commands.  Nothing is
guessed --- what you see is what @command{gc} reports, and @kbd{g}
re-reads it.  gascity never talks to the Gas City supervisor's HTTP
API; where the CLI has no surface for something, the view leaves it
out (@pxref{Capability Gaps}).

@cindex store
@cindex payload store
Reads go through a per-city @dfn{store}: one cached payload per
@command{gc} command line, shared by every view of the city, with a
short freshness window per kind of read (five seconds for
@code{gc status}).  Opening a second view of the same city is instant,
two views asking for the same read cost one process, and a view keeps
showing its last good data while a refresh is in flight.

@cindex non-blocking
Nothing blocks Emacs.  Every read, and every action that needs no more
input from you, runs as a background process with a deadline; the
command you typed returns at once (@pxref{Actions}).

@node What you get
@section What you get

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{A cockpit} (@pxref{The Cockpit}) --- one short triage buffer
per city: what needs you, what is moving, who is working, what is
queued, what just happened, and the rigs.
@item
@strong{Agents and runs} (@pxref{Agents}, @pxref{Runs and Live Events}) --- every
agent as a table or a tree, every workflow run with its step ladder,
and each of them in detail.
@item
@strong{A rig dashboard} (@pxref{The Rig Dashboard}) --- one rig, with
its agents, work, orders and Dolt.
@item
@strong{Live refresh} (@pxref{Live Refresh}) --- one event stream per
city; views refresh when something changes, never on a timer.
@item
@strong{City plumbing} (@pxref{Health Costs and the Lighter}, @pxref{Cities and Remote Cities}, @pxref{Costs})
--- the supervisor, versions, stores and @command{gc doctor}; every
city on every host; recorded usage.
@item
@strong{A mode-line lighter} (@pxref{The Mode-Line Lighter}) --- what
needs you, across every open city, in every mode line.
@item
@strong{Tabulated lists} (@pxref{Tabulated Lists}) --- rigs, sessions,
convoys, orders, mail and Dolt databases, each paginated, sortable and
filterable.
@item
@strong{At-point actions} (@pxref{Actions}) --- nudge, suspend, kill,
wake, drain, sling and more, run on the object under point in the
background and reported in the echo area.
@item
@strong{Remote cities} (@pxref{Remote Cities}) --- every view works
the same on a city opened over TRAMP.
@end itemize

@node Two kinds of buffer
@section Two kinds of buffer

@cindex vui
@cindex tabulated-list-mode
The porcelain is hand-built with two rendering technologies, chosen per
view:

@table @asis
@item vui
A small React-like reconciler drives the heterogeneous
@dfn{dashboards} --- the cockpit, the agent, run and rig detail views,
the Runs view and Health --- where sections of different shapes are stacked and
refreshed independently.
@item @code{tabulated-list-mode}
The built-in Emacs facility drives the homogeneous @dfn{lists}, where
every row has the same columns.
@end table

@cindex beads.el
Issue (@dfn{bead}) display itself is delegated to a sibling package,
@code{beads.el}: when you press @key{RET} on a bead, gascity opens it in
beads.el, scoped to the right rig's store.  gascity is the city-level
porcelain; beads.el is the issue-level porcelain.  The delegation is
one-way: beads.el never requires gascity.el.  Loading gascity extends
beads.el's own sling transient with @command{gc} targets and a
@command{gc} dispatch backend, so @kbd{S} on an issue works without
leaving the beads buffer (@pxref{Slinging from beads}).

