@node Sling and Dispatch
@chapter Sling and Dispatch
@cindex sling
@cindex dispatching work
@fnindex gascity-sling-dispatch
@kindex S

The @dfn{sling} is gascity's one command for putting work in front of an
@dfn{agent}.  @kbd{S} from any gascity view --- or @kbd{M-x
gascity-sling-dispatch} anywhere --- opens @code{gascity-sling-dispatch},
a single adaptive
menu that routes a @dfn{bead}, a @dfn{formula} run, or freeform task text
to the agent that should take it (@pxref{Terminology}).  The menu asks only the questions context
has not already answered: with a bead at point and a remembered
target, the whole dispatch is two keystrokes, @kbd{S} then @kbd{s},
with zero prompts.  A cold menu walks six conceptual stages with
typed completion:

@example
What → Who → How → Preview → Launch → Follow
@end example

@noindent
What is the work, Who is the agent that takes it, How is the picked
formula's variables, Preview is the always-on summary plus the
on-demand full picture, Launch runs @command{gc sling} asynchronously,
and Follow is the momentary offer to jump to the workflow a launch
created.  The menu works the same over TRAMP (@pxref{Remote
Cities}): every reader keeps the city the menu was opened for.


@menu
* The sling shapes::  One menu, three shapes.
* The sling menu::  What, who and how.
* Picking work::  Beads, convoys and free text.
* Picking a formula::  The formula and its variables.
* Picking the target::  The agent or rig.
* Typed variables::  Generated infix keys.
* The live footer::  A summary of the dispatch.
* Validation warnings::  Traps and how to avoid them.
* The preview buffer::  The full dispatch, before launch.
* Launch and follow::  Start the run and follow it.
* Remembered state::  The sling remembers your choices.
* Sling key summary::  Every sling key.
* Slinging from beads::  The same sling from a beads.el issue buffer.
* Route Work::  Hand a bead, a formula or free text to an agent.
* Dispatch a Formula::  The sling menu, its shapes and its keys.
@end menu

@node The sling shapes
@section The sling shapes
@cindex sling shapes
@cindex shape inference

@command{gc sling} comes in three forms, and the menu infers which one
you mean from the work and the formula you select.  There is no shape
flag and no toggle key:

@table @asis
@item Plain
@code{gc sling @var{agent} @var{bead-or-text}}: hand an agent a bead,
or a task described in free text.  The header sentence reads
@samp{Sling bead bl-5ja to mayor}.
@item Formula
@option{--formula}: run a formula itself on the target agent, with no
work attached.  @samp{Run pancakes (formula) on mayor}.
@item Targeted
@option{--on}: run a formula against a bead --- the formula's steps
drain the bead.  @samp{Run build-basic against bead bl-5ja, drained
by hello-world/gc.implementation-worker}.
@end table

The shape is a pure function of two answers.  No formula picked is
the plain route.  A formula picked with work in scope is the targeted
@option{--on} shape; without work, the targetless @option{--formula}
shape.  Picking a formula (@pxref{Picking a formula}) flips the
header sentence immediately --- the sentence @emph{is} the shape
display.  A formula whose steps drain the bead renders the
@samp{drained by} clause above; one that does not renders @samp{on}
the target instead (@samp{Run build-basic against bead bl-5ja, on
mayor}).

The sentence is display only: the dispatch itself follows the picked
formula's own convoy rule, so a formula that requires a convoy with no
work chosen never gets a new prompt --- it surfaces as a footer
warning (@pxref{Validation warnings}).

The shape also decides what the menu shows.  The formula's typed
variables (@pxref{Typed variables}) appear only once a formula is
picked.  The routing flags render only on the plain shape
(@pxref{The sling menu}) --- @command{gc} consumes the whole group
only there --- except the two flags carved out for a targeted formula
shape: @option{-n} nudge and @option{-t} wisp root title, which
the formula shapes (@option{--formula} and @option{--on}) forward
too.

@node The sling menu
@section The sling menu
@cindex sling menu

The menu is a stack of full-width groups.  The first group carries
the city's title, the one-sentence header, and the live footer
(@pxref{The live footer}) --- the summary is always on screen and
recomputes as each answer changes.  Then one group per stage, then
the actions:

@table @asis
@item What
The work and the formula: @kbd{A} picks the work (@pxref{Picking
work}), @kbd{f} the formula (@pxref{Picking a formula}).
@item Who
The target agent: @kbd{T} (@pxref{Picking the target}).
@item How
The picked formula's typed variables (@pxref{Typed variables}), full
width, and absent without a formula.
@item Routing flags
The full group --- @option{-c} skip auto-convoy, @option{-a} reassign
(clear the human assignee), @option{-n} nudge the target after
routing, @option{-m} merge strategy, @option{-t} wisp root title ---
renders only on the plain shape.  A targeted formula shape renders
just the two flags carved out for it: @option{-n} and @option{-t}.
@item Actions
@kbd{s} launch, @kbd{P} full preview, @kbd{r} recipe preview,
@kbd{g} refresh formulas, @kbd{x} reset, @kbd{q} quit.
@end table

An answered stage collapses to its one line: the answer is visible,
and the binding stays live, so the key that answered it changes it
again.  An unanswered stage shows its pick hint.  With a bead at
point and a remembered target, every stage holds an answer:

@shot{sling-plain-dark, The sling menu pre-seeded for a plain dispatch (ef-deuteranopia-dark)}

Pressing @kbd{S} with nothing pre-seeded shows the hints instead ---
and the header sentence still reads as one line:

@shot{sling-cold-dark, A cold sling entry with nothing pre-seeded (ef-deuteranopia-dark)}

@node Picking work
@section Picking work
@cindex work picker

@kbd{A} answers the What stage's work slot.  It is a completing read
over the city's open, in-progress and blocked beads plus its
convoys, each annotated with its title, status and store:

@example
Work (bead or convoy; RET-empty or C-u for freeform text): bl-5ja
  bl-5ja    e2e sling-v2: verify unified plain sling over TRAMP · open · city
  bl-70ac   e2e demo latch — gce-e2e demo · open · city
  hw-3fd    … · in_progress · hello-world
@end example

Two escapes cover what the roster cannot list: a prefix argument
(@kbd{C-u A}) goes straight to freeform text, and @key{RET} on empty
input falls through to the same text prompt (@samp{Bead id or task
text:}).  Freeform text becomes the plain shape's task text.  A bead
or convoy at point pre-seeds the slot before the menu even opens ---
that is where the @kbd{S} @kbd{s} fast path gets its work.

@node Picking a formula
@section Picking a formula
@cindex formula picker

@kbd{f} picks from the union of the local formula catalog and the
city's @code{gc formula list}, annotated: the description where the
catalog has one, @samp{(city)} for city-local formulas, @samp{(not
in catalog)} for the rest.  The catalog refreshes asynchronously
behind the menu; @kbd{g} forces a refresh now.

@example
Formula: pancakes
  build-basic       Run the default Gas City full-lifecycle build …
  pancakes          Make pancakes from scratch
  e2e-demo          (city)
  mol-extra         (not in catalog)
@end example

Picking a formula is also how the shape changes (@pxref{The sling
shapes}): with work in scope the run is targeted (@option{--on});
without work, targetless (@option{--formula}).  A formula that
declares no variables --- @samp{pancakes} --- keeps the How stage out
of the menu entirely; the flow is What, Who, @kbd{s}.

@shot{sling-formula-dark, The formula shape with no work attached (ef-deuteranopia-dark)}

@node Picking the target
@section Picking the target
@cindex target picker
@cindex derived target

@kbd{T} answers Who.  Completion runs over @emph{agents}, not
sessions --- city agents first, then one group per rig, each
candidate annotated with its scope and live state:

@example
Target agent: hello-world/gc.implementation-worker
  mayor                                    city · active
  pi                                       city · provider pi
  hello-world/gc.implementation-worker     hello-world · derived default
  hello-world/gc.run-operator              hello-world · stopped
  hello-world/gc.requirements-planner      hello-world · idle
@end example

Free entry still works: a typed name is used verbatim, so a cold
roster never dead-ends the menu.

The default is derived, and you press @kbd{T} only to disagree with
it.  The derivation, in order:

@enumerate
@item
The work bead's rig declares a @samp{default_sling_target} (or
@samp{default_sling_targets}) --- in its city.toml, when gascity can
resolve the rig.
@item
Per-(city, formula) target memory: the target you last launched this
formula with on this city, recorded at launch.
@item
The implementation-worker convention: when the roster confirms
exactly one rig-scoped @code{gc.implementation-worker}, it is the
target for build formulas.
@end enumerate

A derived target shows with a @samp{derived} tag in the header and
is the @kbd{T} picker's initial input --- @key{RET} keeps it --- and
@kbd{s} uses it without prompting.  When nothing is derivable the
header says so, and @kbd{s} prompts for a target instead of guessing.

@node Typed variables
@section Typed variables
@cindex typed variables
@cindex variables, formula

The How group appears full width once a formula is picked: one infix
per declared variable, titled @samp{How — @var{formula} vars}.  A
formula that declares more variables than
@code{gascity-sling--max-inline-vars} renders only that many rows and
folds the rest under a @samp{…  (@var{N} more vars)} hint; the folded
variables stay live (their keys and seeded values still dispatch) and
@kbd{C-x a} expands the rows in place.  Each
variable reads with the reader it calls for, so an illegal value is
unrepresentable at entry:

@table @asis
@item File
@code{context_path} and every @code{*_path} read with file
completion relative to the target rig's workdir --- TRAMP-safe
(@pxref{Remote Cities}).
@item Directory
@code{artifact_root} reads with directory completion, seeded with
@file{plans/@var{slug}/} where the slug derives from the work bead's
@emph{title} --- lower-cased, runs of non-alphanumerics collapsed to
one dash, the house convention behind @file{plans/dashboard-v3/} ---
never from the bare bead id.  Freeform work text, or the formula
name, seeds it when there is no bead.  The slug is independent of the
routing flag @option{-t/--title}: that flag names the routed wisp
root bead and never re-seeds @code{artifact_root}, so renaming the
root bead cannot move the plans directory.
@item Agent
Every @code{*_target} reads with the same roster completion as
@kbd{T} (@pxref{Picking the target}); @code{implementation_target}
seeds from the chosen target's rig.
@item Numeric
Numbers validate digits and refuse a bad entry before any
@command{gc} call: @samp{Var max_iterations must be numeric (got
lots)}.
@item Choice
Enum, methodology and pattern variables offer their restricted
choices --- @samp{separate|same-session} --- plus the
(formula, variable) pair's own history, so a value @command{gc}
would reject cannot be entered.
@item Bool
Boolean variables toggle on their key press.
@item String
Anything unrecognized fails soft to plain string entry.
@end table

@noindent
Two more conventions fill the common cases: @code{rig_name}
auto-derives from the chosen target's rig and stays editable like
any other answer, and a variable whose declared default is all digits
--- or whose name follows the @code{max_*} or @code{*_iterations}
convention --- reads as numeric.  The heuristics are overridable:
an alist mapping variable name to a reader function
(@code{gascity-sling-var-readers}) wins over every convention above.

Variable keys are assigned deterministically and avoid the reserved
single letters (@pxref{Sling key summary}), so a formula's variables
bind the same keys in every session.

@shot{sling-on-dark, The targeted shape with its typed How group (ef-deuteranopia-dark)}

@node The live footer
@section The live footer
@cindex live footer

The footer is the menu's second header line.  It is always visible,
and it recomputes as each answer changes.  It carries the launch
summary --- the shape, the target with its scope, the count of set
variables --- and the client-side validation status:

@example
  ✓ Ready — plain route · target mayor (city) · no vars
  ✓ Ready — formula run · target mayor (city) · 0 vars
  ✓ Ready — on run · target rig-scoped · 6 of 18 vars set
@end example

@noindent
or the reason it has something to say:

@example
  ⚠ No work chosen — pick work before launching
@end example

The footer renders from cached data only: nothing on the menu's
render path runs @command{gc}.  A warning never blocks @kbd{s} ---
@command{gc} remains the authority --- but it is unmissable, and the
full detail is one @kbd{P} away (@pxref{The preview buffer}).

@node Validation warnings
@section Validation warnings
@cindex bl-bdj trap
@cindex cross-store route

Two of @command{gc}'s known failure modes are caught client-side,
before launch:

@table @asis
@item The formulas-v2 target trap
A v2 formula whose steps name binding-qualified run targets ---
@code{gc.run-operator}, not @code{run-operator} --- needs a
@emph{rig-scoped} target.  The same dispatch to a city-scoped agent
fails at instantiation with @samp{unknown formulas v2 target}; the
dry run does not exercise the trap, so the client warns.  With the
city-scoped @code{mayor} as target the footer reads:

@example
  ⚠ formulas v2 target: this formula needs a rig-scoped target — the chosen
    city agent will fail with "unknown formulas v2 target" (bl-bdj); pick a
    hello-world/* agent with T
@end example

@noindent
and with a rig-scoped agent it reads @samp{✓ Ready — on run · target
rig-scoped · @dots{}}.  (@samp{bl-bdj} names the 2026-09-16 incident
that taught this warning.)

@item Cross-store routes
@command{gc} refuses a route whose work bead and target agent read
different stores --- a bead in the hello-world store cannot route to
an agent of another rig.  The footer warns first:

@example
  ⚠ cross-store route: bead hw-ab12 lives in the hello-world store but the
    target reads the gascity.el store — gc will refuse (pick a city agent or
    a hello-world agent)
@end example
@end table

Missing pieces warn the same way: a convoy-requiring formula with no
work (@samp{build-basic drains a bead — pick work with A}), missing
required variables (@samp{Missing required vars: artifact_root}), and
no target at all (@samp{No target — T to choose, or s will prompt}).

@node The preview buffer
@section The preview buffer
@cindex preview buffer

@kbd{P} opens the full preview: everything gascity can compute
client-side renders at once, and @command{gc}'s routing plan --- from
@command{gc sling @dots{} --dry-run} --- fills in when it answers.
First paint never blocks on the dry run.  The buffer opens through
the same host-qualified, city-pinned machinery as every gascity view
(@pxref{Remote buffers}), titled @samp{Sling preview — @var{CITY}},
and holds:

@itemize @bullet
@item
The header sentence, exactly as the menu shows it.
@item
A @emph{Validation} section: every client-side check, full text,
passing or warning.
@item
A @emph{Recipe} section: the formula's steps and what each needs ---
the recipe DAG, from the cached recipe.
@item
A @emph{Routing plan} section: which agent or session gets each step,
from the dry run.
@end itemize

@kbd{s} launches directly from the preview buffer; @kbd{q} closes
it.  @kbd{r} keeps its old job, the server-substituted recipe
preview.  The preview is never a gate: @kbd{s} works in the menu at
any time, warning or not.

@shot{sling-preview-dark, The P full preview buffer (ef-deuteranopia-dark)}

@node Launch and follow
@section Launch and follow
@cindex follow offer

@kbd{s} launches.  The call runs asynchronously like every gascity
action (@pxref{Actions}): the menu closes, the echo area
reports, and the views the launch touched re-read.

A plain route echoes the routing (@samp{Routed bl-5ja to mayor}) ---
no workflow is created, so there is nothing to follow.  A formula
launch echoes the workflow it created and offers to jump to it:

@example
Launched workflow bl-9xyz (build-basic on bl-5ja) — F: run view
@end example

@noindent
The offer is momentary: @kbd{F} opens the run view
(@code{gascity-run-show}) on the created workflow's root bead
(@pxref{Runs and Live Events}), and any other key dismisses the offer.  Nothing is
yanked away --- you stay where you are unless you take it.

@node Remembered state
@section Remembered state
@cindex sling history
@cindex target memory

The menu keeps three kinds of state, all scoped per city and all
preserved across openings:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Per-(formula, variable) history: the choices made for this formula
before, offered by every typed reader.
@item
Per-(city, formula) target memory: the target of the last real launch
of this formula on this city.  It is recorded at launch and drives
the derived default (@pxref{Picking the target}).
@item
The remembered menu state: work, formula, target and values, so the
menu re-opens with every answered stage still answered.
@kbd{x} clears all four and forgets them; a real launch forgets them
too.
@end itemize

The menu also keeps the city it was opened for (@pxref{Remote
Cities}): completion, the roster, and the file and directory readers
all keep reading that city, so a menu opened for a remote city never
completes against the local one.

@node Sling key summary
@section Sling key summary

@example
A   Work (open beads + convoys; C-u for freeform text; point pre-seeds)
f   Formula (with work the run is targeted; without it targetless)
T   Target (agents grouped by rig; the derived default heads the header)
--  one typed infix per declared formula var (file, directory, agent,
    numeric, choice, bool; keys deterministic, outside the reserved set;
    vars beyond the inline cap fold under an ellipsis row, live but
    hidden until C-x a)
s   Launch (anytime — the preview is never a gate)
P   Full preview buffer (validation, recipe DAG, routing plan)
r   Recipe preview (server-side substituted, as today)
g   Refresh formulas
x   Reset (clear work, formula, target, vars)
q   Quit
@end example

@noindent
The generated variable keys avoid the reserved single letters
@samp{A f T c a n m t s P r g x q} --- @kbd{p} is free, the preview
moved to @kbd{P}.

@node Slinging from beads
@section Slinging from beads.el
@cindex beads sling
@cindex slinging a bead
@kindex S
@fnindex beads-sling-for-issue
@fnindex beads-sling-dispatch

beads.el owns the bead list and detail buffers, and it owns their sling
entry point.  With gascity loaded, @kbd{S} in a beads show buffer, epic
status, dependency tree or wisp list opens beads.el's
@code{beads-sling-for-issue} transient with the issue at point already
selected as the work.  (In the plain beads list @kbd{S} stays
@code{beads-list-sort}, and in the agent list it stays
@code{beads-agent-list-stop-all}.)  Selecting a target and launching
runs the same @command{gc sling} the gascity menu runs; you never leave
the beads buffer.

The Who picker gains gascity's qualified @command{gc} targets from the
cached @command{gc agent list} roster --- @samp{rig/agent} for a rig
agent and the bare name for a city-level one --- alongside beads.el's
local targets.  They are marked with the @samp{gc} backend.  All sling
shapes work from the beads transient: a plain dispatch runs
@code{gc sling TARGET BEAD}, a formula with work runs
@code{gc sling TARGET BEAD --on FORMULA}, and a formula-only dispatch
runs @code{gc sling TARGET FORMULA --formula}.  Every shape carries a
resolved target, because @command{gc} refuses @code{--formula} without
one.  Every variable that was actually set is passed as
@code{--var name=value}; @command{gc sling} has no prompt flag, so a
freeform instruction is dropped with a message rather than put in the
argv.  The transient's advisory warnings (cross-store route,
city-scoped target on a formulas-v2 recipe, missing target, missing
required variable) are the same advisory checks the gascity footer
shows and never block a valid launch; @command{gc} remains the
authority.

@kbd{?} in a beads buffer also gains a @strong{City} group from
gascity: @kbd{E} opens the cockpit, @kbd{J} the rig dashboard at point,
@kbd{K} a chosen rig dashboard, @kbd{A} the agents table and @kbd{Q}
the gascity sling menu.  Bead action bars gain an additive @kbd{V}
cockpit entry, bound in both bead maps; gascity does not shadow a
beads.el key.  A beads mutation
(closing, claiming or updating a bead) refreshes gascity's cached
bead-derived views once.

@quotation Note
Cold start: gascity can name a city only when it already knows one ---
from @code{gascity-context-city}, from a known city whose rig contains
the beads buffer's repository, or from a city a previous dispatch used.
In a fresh Emacs with no gascity view open, the Who picker therefore
shows only beads.el's local targets until a city becomes known; open a
gascity view or set @code{gascity-context-city} to seed it.  Nothing on
that path spawns @command{gc} or blocks.
@end quotation

Limits: gascity adds no new CLI or server behaviour here.  beads.el owns
the bead list and detail UI, the dispatch menu and the @kbd{S} transient;
gascity only registers the @command{gc} targets, backend, advisories,
menu group and refresh hook through beads.el's public seams.  The one
registration point is @file{lisp/gascity-beads-sling.el}.  The
gascity-side ownership record is @file{docs/cross-repo-ownership.md}.

@node Route Work
@section Route Work

Routing work means putting a bead, a formula run or free task text in
front of the agent that should take it.  gascity has one command for
it: the sling, @kbd{S} from any view (or @kbd{M-x
gascity-sling-dispatch} anywhere).  @xref{Sling and Dispatch}.

The menu asks what, who and how, in typed completion: @kbd{A} picks the
work (beads and convoys; @kbd{C-u} for free text), @kbd{f} a formula,
@kbd{T} the target agent, the generated infix keys fill the formula's
variables, and @kbd{s} launches.  With a bead at point and a remembered
target, @kbd{S} @kbd{s} dispatches with no prompts at all; @kbd{P}
opens the full preview first.  @xref{Sling key summary}.

Beads themselves are managed in beads.el.  @kbd{j b} opens the beads of
the rig at point (or a chosen rig) in beads.el, and @kbd{c} opens the
bead-actions menu; ready work also shows in the cockpit's @strong{Work}
section and the rig dashboard's @strong{Ready} section
(@pxref{The ? dispatch}, @pxref{The Cockpit}, @pxref{The Rig Dashboard}).

@node Dispatch a Formula
@section Dispatch a Formula

A formula is a reusable workflow; dispatching it to an agent (or
against a bead) is the sling's job.  @kbd{S} from any view, or
@kbd{M-x gascity-sling-dispatch}, opens the one adaptive menu.
@xref{Sling and Dispatch}.

The shape is inferred, never flagged: work with no formula is the plain
route, a formula with work is the targeted @option{--on} shape, and a
formula without work is the targetless @option{--formula} shape.
@kbd{A} picks work, @kbd{f} picks the formula (whose declared variables
become typed infix keys), @kbd{T} picks the target, the live footer
summarizes the dispatch and warns about traps, @kbd{s} launches (the
preview is never a gate), @kbd{P} opens the full preview and the
momentary @kbd{F} follows the run a launch created.
@xref{The sling shapes}, @xref{Sling key summary}.

@c ====================================================================
@node Actions
@chapter Actions
@cindex actions
@cindex mutating commands

Beyond reading, gascity dispatches @command{gc}'s mutating commands.
Every action is reachable two ways: @dfn{at point} (a key acts on the
rig, agent, mail or order under point) and @dfn{by prompt} (from the
dispatcher's sub-transients or @kbd{M-x}, reading arguments with
completion).

@section Actions never block

@cindex asynchronous actions
@cindex pending action
@cindex @file{*gascity-log*}
An action gathers what it needs from you first --- a confirmation, a
nudge message in the minibuffer, a transient, a compose buffer --- and
then only @emph{starts} the @command{gc} call and returns.  You can keep
working while it runs:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Pending.} The row the action is about shows @samp{@dots{}} in
its status slot (the glyph of a vui row, the status column of a list)
until @command{gc} answers.
@item
@strong{Success.} The echo area says what happened ---
@samp{Suspended beads.el/requirements-planner-1} --- and the views the
action touched re-read.
@item
@strong{Failure.} The echo area shows the first line of @command{gc}'s
error; the full error goes to the city's
@file{*gascity-log:@: CITY*} buffer (@file{*gascity-log:@:
CITY@@host*} for a remote city).  A failure never pops up a modal
error.
@item
@strong{Ordering.} Actions on the same target run one after the other;
actions on different targets run at the same time.  Killing a view
does not cancel an action in flight: it still finishes and reports.
@item
@strong{Deadline.} Every call is killed after
@code{gascity-remote-async-timeout} seconds (@pxref{Remote Cities}),
locally as well as remotely.
@end itemize

@noindent
Destructive actions --- kill, reset, rig restart, city start and stop,
@command{gc doctor --fix} --- confirm first; the quick ones (nudge,
suspend, wake, drain, undrain) don't.  City start and stop keep a
buffer streaming their long-running output.

@section Session actions

@cindex nudge
@cindex suspend
@cindex kill
@cindex drain
@cindex peek
Run at point in the dashboards and the session list, or by prompt from
@kbd{A} in the dispatcher (@code{gascity-session-dispatch}):
@fnindex gascity-session-dispatch

@table @asis
@item Nudge --- @kbd{M}
@code{gc session nudge}: send the agent a message (prompts for it).  The
ephemeral, zero-overhead way to signal an agent.
@item Suspend --- @kbd{s}
@code{gc session suspend}: stop the agent's runtime; its bead persists.
@item Kill --- @kbd{K}
@code{gc session kill}: force-kill the runtime (the reconciler may
restart it).  Confirms first.
@item Wake --- @kbd{w}
@code{gc session wake}: wake a suspended agent.
@item Drain --- @kbd{D}
@code{gc runtime drain}: wind the agent down gracefully --- it finishes
its current task, then exits.  Distinct from suspend and kill.
@item Undrain --- @kbd{U}
@code{gc runtime undrain}: cancel a drain.
@item Reset --- @kbd{R}
@code{gc session reset}: restart the agent's session fresh.  Confirms first.
@item Peek --- @kbd{v}
@code{gc session peek}: show the agent's recent terminal output in a
read-only buffer.
@end table

@cindex pool slot
These verbs, and @kbd{t}, act on the agent's session.  A pool slot
(@samp{bd.dog-1}) has none until work arrives and gc starts it, so on
such a row they say so (@samp{bd.dog-1 has no session; pool slots start
when work arrives}) instead of failing in gc; its drawer says the same.

@section Rig actions

By prompt from @kbd{R} in the dispatcher (@code{gascity-rig-dispatch}), or
at point in the rig list:
@fnindex gascity-rig-dispatch

@table @asis
@item Suspend --- @kbd{s}
@code{gc rig suspend}.
@item Resume --- @kbd{r}
@code{gc rig resume}.
@item Restart --- @kbd{R}
@code{gc rig restart}.  Confirms, as it kills the rig's agent sessions.
@end table

@section Dispatch and lifecycle

@cindex sling
@cindex order
@cindex city lifecycle
@table @asis
@item Sling --- dispatcher @kbd{S}
@fnindex gascity-sling-dispatch
@code{gc sling}: route a bead id, a formula, or free task text to an
agent --- one unified, adaptive menu covering all three shapes
(@pxref{Sling and Dispatch}).
@item Run order --- dispatcher @kbd{O}, list @kbd{x}
@fnindex gascity-order-run
@code{gc order run}: run an order now, bypassing its trigger.
@item City lifecycle --- dispatcher @kbd{L}
@fnindex gascity-start
@fnindex gascity-stop
@code{gc start} and @code{gc stop} start and stop the whole city.  Both
confirm first and stream their (long-running) output; a prefix argument to
stop forces a hard, graceless shutdown.
@end table

@section Attached terminals

@cindex attach
@cindex scrolling a terminal
@cindex copy-mode
@kbd{t} on an agent (@code{gascity-tmux-at-point}) opens a terminal buffer
holding a live @command{tmux} client.  A tmux client draws on the
@dfn{alternate screen}, so the Emacs buffer contains only what is on
screen right now: the history is tmux's, and the backend's own scrollback
and copy commands have nothing to show.  Scrolling therefore goes through
tmux:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{C-b [}
Enter tmux's copy-mode.  @kbd{C-b} is the prefix on Gas City's per-city
tmux servers; a personal @file{~/.tmux.conf} elsewhere may rebind it.
@item @key{PageUp} / @key{PageDown}
Page through the history once in copy-mode --- and @kbd{C-b @key{PageUp}}
enters copy-mode a page back in one go.  @kbd{M-v} and @kbd{C-v} do the
same, and the arrow keys move a line at a time.
@item @kbd{q}
Leave copy-mode and return to the live pane.
@end table

@cindex scroll sub-mode
@cindex Emacs keys, in a terminal
@fnindex gascity-terminal-scroll-mode
@fnindex gascity-terminal-scroll-toggle
For Magit-flavoured reading, @kbd{C-c s}
(@code{gascity-terminal-scroll-toggle}) toggles
@code{gascity-terminal-scroll-mode}, a buffer-local scroll sub-mode:
activation enters tmux copy-mode (those are the only bytes the agent
can see on entry), and while it is active the Emacs scroll keys are
translated to copy-mode byte sequences and sent to the pane --- the
agent receives no keys (@kbd{C-p}/@kbd{C-n} scroll a line,
@kbd{C-v}/@kbd{M-v}/@key{PageUp}/@key{PageDown} page, @kbd{M-<}/@kbd{M->}
jump to the top/bottom).  @kbd{q} or @key{ESC} leave copy-mode and
hand the keys back to the agent.  The toggle is optimistic and
self-healing: re-pressing @kbd{C-c s} while the mode thinks it is
active leaves copy-mode first, then re-enters, so an out-of-band exit
(the mouse wheel, below) costs one re-toggle.  The status mirror's
mode-line segment carries a @samp{[scroll]} marker while the sub-mode
is active.

@cindex mouse wheel, terminal
@cindex gascity-terminal-ensure-mouse
@vindex gascity-terminal-ensure-mouse
@defvr {User Option} gascity-terminal-ensure-mouse
The attach pre-step additionally turns the session's tmux @code{mouse}
option on (session-scoped) and installs one copy-mode binding that
leaves copy-mode when the wheel reaches the bottom --- wheeling back
down returns to the live tail.  Both are restored when the terminal
buffer is killed.  Set this variable to nil to keep tmux's defaults.
@end defvr

The wheel itself depends on the backend: ghostel and eat report mouse
events natively, so the wheel scrolls the transcript through tmux
whenever the agent's own interface has not claimed the mouse.  vterm
and term report nothing; there the scroll sub-mode's effective keymap
also carries the wheel --- one notch scrolls about ten lines through
copy-mode, and the first notch after entering re-sends the copy-mode
entry.  On the reporting backends the wheel extension is never
installed, so their native passthrough is untouched.

@cindex bead at point, terminal
@fnindex gascity-bead-show-at-point
Agent transcripts are full of bead ids.  Put point on one --- the
backend's own copy mode, @kbd{C-c C-t} in vterm, frees point from the
terminal cursor --- and Eldoc shows the bead's status and title
(beads.el's @code{beads-eldoc-mode}, looked up asynchronously and scoped
to the rig owning the id's prefix), while @kbd{C-c b}
(@code{gascity-bead-show-at-point}) opens it in beads.el's detail view.
The same command works in any text buffer, a shell or a mail body
included.

These keys only reach tmux because gascity suppresses the global minor
modes named in @code{gascity-terminal-unshadow-minor-modes} inside its
terminal buffers; a minor-mode map outranks the terminal backend's own
keymap, so without that, @code{pixel-scroll-precision-mode} (for
instance) swallows @key{PageUp} and @key{PageDown} and scrolls the Emacs
window --- which has nothing above the visible screen to scroll to.

The mouse wheel is a tmux setting, not an Emacs one: it scrolls the
history only where the city's tmux server runs with @code{mouse on}
(@code{set -g mouse on}), which is not the default.  How far back you can
scroll is tmux's @code{history-limit} --- 2000 lines unless the city says
otherwise.

