@node Health Costs and the Lighter
@chapter Health, Costs and the Lighter
@cindex health
@fnindex gascity-health
@kindex j h

@kbd{j h} (or @kbd{M-x gascity-health}) opens
@file{*gascity-health:@: CITY*} in @code{gascity-health-mode}: the
city's plumbing, one section per @command{gc} read so that one failure
never blanks the others.

@example
Health  bright-lights                                         ▲ watch
Supervisor
  pid 22262 · supervisor · ▲ degraded (no_agents_running) · not suspended
Versions
  gc 1.4.2 · bd 1.3.0 · dolt 2.3.5 · store NativeDoltStore
Store  ◐
  ~/bright-lights/.beads/dolt        109 MB   threshold 1 MB/row
  ◐ store health: runtime status probe incomplete
  ● dolt :41586 · pid 23571 · 120 ms · 2 databases
  size (this session)  ▁▁▂▂▃
Rig stores  1
  ● hello-world    ~/hello-world                              dolt ok
Doctor  88 passed · 5 warned · 0 failed · ran 4m ago   ! run  F run --fix
  ▲ formula-requirements         blocking  36 formula requirement warning(s)
  ▸ 88 checks ok
@end example

@shot{health-dark, Health of emacs-city (ef-deuteranopia-dark)}

@section What it shows

@table @asis
@item The top line
The city (with @samp{@@host} for a remote one) and an overall verdict:
@samp{■ attention} when the controller is down, the city is unusable,
the Dolt server is not running, a doctor check failed or a rig store
cannot be read; @samp{▲ watch} when the city is degraded, the store is
over its size threshold, @command{gc} reports partial errors, or
doctor warned; else @samp{● ok}.  While the host is unreachable it
reads @samp{○ offline @@host}.
@item Supervisor
From @code{gc status}: the controller's pid and mode, health
(@samp{health ok}, @samp{▲ degraded (@var{signals})} or @samp{■
unusable}) and whether the city is suspended.
@item Versions
@code{gc version}, and the beads store kind from @code{gc status}.  The
@command{bd} and @command{dolt} versions appear once doctor has run:
its report is the only place @command{gc} exposes them.
@item Store
From @code{gc status}: the store's path, size, rows-per-megabyte ratio
and threshold, a @samp{▲} line when the store is over it and a
@samp{◐} line per partial error.  From @code{gc dolt health}: the Dolt
server's port, pid, latency and database count, with @samp{▲} lines for
orphan databases, zombie processes or stale backups; @key{RET} on the
server line opens the Dolt list.  Then @samp{size (this session)}, a
@samp{▁▂▃} sparkline of the store sizes sampled this session (every
status read the cockpit or this view got; the tooltip gives the range).
@item Rig stores
One @code{gc rig status @var{name}} per rig: @samp{● dolt ok} when the
rig's beads are initialized and its Dolt database exists, @samp{▲} with
the reason otherwise, @samp{○ suspended}, or @samp{■} with the error for
a rig whose read failed.  @key{SPC} shows the rig's prefix, branch and
agents; @key{RET} opens its dashboard; @kbd{l} its git log.
@item Doctor
The last @command{gc doctor} report of the session: counts and how long
ago it ran.  Failed checks, then warnings, get a row each ---
@samp{■ @var{name}  @var{severity}  @var{message}} --- and @key{SPC}
opens a check's fix hint and details.  Passing checks fold into
@samp{▸ N checks ok}.
@end table

@section Keys

@table @kbd
@item !
@kindex ! @r{(health)}
@fnindex gascity-health-doctor
Run @command{gc doctor} in the background.  It is never run on open or
on @kbd{g} --- it takes tens of seconds --- so the section reads
@samp{not run} until you ask.  While it runs the header reads
@samp{running@dots{}} and the rest of the view stays usable; the report
is cached for the session, so reopening the view shows it with its age.
Doctor has its own deadline, @code{gascity-health-doctor-timeout}.
@item F
@kindex F @r{(health)}
@fnindex gascity-health-doctor-fix
Run @command{gc doctor --fix} after a confirmation, in the background,
the same way.
@item g
@kindex g @r{(health)}
@fnindex gascity-health-refresh
Re-read every section but Doctor.
@item @key{RET}
@kindex RET @r{(health)}
@fnindex gascity-health-activate
On a rig, open its dashboard; on the Store header or Dolt line, the Dolt
list; on the Rig stores header, the rig list; on the Doctor header, run
doctor.
@end table

@menu
* Costs::  Recorded usage by run.
* The Mode-Line Lighter::  Needs-you totals in every mode line.
@end menu

@node Costs
@section Costs
@cindex costs
@fnindex gascity-costs
@kindex j $

@kbd{j $} (or @kbd{M-x gascity-costs}) shows @command{gc costs} ---
model tokens and compute wall-seconds recorded per run, with a
list-price estimate --- in a read-only buffer,
@file{*gascity-costs:@: CITY*}.  @command{gc} prints it as a text table
and has no JSON form of it, so the text is shown exactly as @command{gc}
prints it.  When a run records no usage (zero invocations), the view adds
a note under the table saying so: a @samp{0.0000} estimate means no
usage was recorded, not that the run was free, and the note points at
@env{GC_DISABLE_USAGE_METRICS} as the first thing to check.  It is read
in the background; @kbd{g} (@code{gascity-costs-refresh}) re-reads it and
the header line says how long ago it did.

@shot{costs-dark, The Costs view --- recorded usage by run (ef-deuteranopia-dark)}

@node The Mode-Line Lighter
@section The Mode-Line Lighter
@cindex mode-line lighter
@cindex lighter
@fnindex gascity-mode-line-mode

@code{gascity-mode-line-mode} is a global minor mode, off by default,
that shows what needs you in every open city in every mode line:

@example
 GC[bl ▲1 · ec ■1▲2]
@end example

@shot{lighter-dark, The lighter in the mode line (ef-deuteranopia-dark)}

@noindent
One segment per open cockpit: the city's initials (@samp{emacs-city}
@result{} @samp{ec}; a remote one adds @samp{@@host}), then its Needs
you totals --- @samp{■} attention and @samp{▲} watch --- or @samp{●}
when it needs nothing.  @kbd{mouse-1} on a segment shows that city's
cockpit; the tooltip spells the counts out.  Killing a cockpit drops its
segment.

The counts are the ones each cockpit publishes when it renders
(@pxref{The Cockpit}).  The lighter never runs @command{gc} and never
touches a remote host: its mode-line construct is a string rebuilt only
when a count changes, so redisplay does no work at all.

@lisp
(gascity-mode-line-mode 1)
@end lisp

@noindent
With live refresh (@pxref{Live Refresh}) the cockpit re-renders when an
event arrives, so the lighter follows the city's event stream.

@c ====================================================================
@node Bookmarks
@chapter Bookmarks
@cindex bookmarks

Every gascity view is an Emacs bookmark: @kbd{C-x r m}
(@code{bookmark-set}) in the cockpit, a rig dashboard, the Agents table
or tree, an agent's detail, Runs, a run's detail, Events, the mail inbox
or a thread, Health, Cities, costs, or any of the lists, and @kbd{C-x r
b} (@code{bookmark-jump}), @code{bookmark-bmenu-list} or
@code{consult-bookmark} opens it again.

The default name says what the view shows and where:

@example
gascity: bright-lights
gascity: bright-lights@@localhost
gascity-runs: bright-lights
gascity-run: hw-1a2b@@localhost
gascity-rig: hello-world
@end example

@noindent
The record keeps the view, the city directory the view is pinned to
(local, or its TRAMP name for a remote city), the view's object (rig,
run, agent, thread) and its filters.  Making it reads buffer state
only; nothing runs, locally or on the host.  A thread's bookmark keeps
its first message's headers, never a body.

@shot{bookmarks-dark, The bookmark list with two fixture gascity views (ef-deuteranopia-dark)}

@fnindex gascity-bookmark-jump
A jump opens the view the usual way, in its city: at once, @samp{…}
while its reads run, the offline state when the host is unreachable
(@pxref{Scheduling}).  The recorded city root is remembered first, so
even the first jump to a remote city walks nothing over TRAMP.  The
view's filters are then set as they were recorded.

