@node Worked Examples
@chapter Worked Examples

@cindex worked examples
@cindex walkthroughs
The task guide lists what each view is for.  This chapter walks three
jobs from start to finish, with the exact keys.  Each example assumes a
running city and a gascity view opened from inside it
(@pxref{Install and Start}).

@menu
* Route a bead through a workflow::  From ready bead to a running run.
* Recover a stalled agent::          Find it, read it, nudge or reset it.
* Open and drive a remote city::     Work on another host over TRAMP.
@end menu

@node Route a bead through a workflow
@section Route a bead through a workflow

@cindex route work, example
@cindex sling, example
A bead is ready in beads.el and you want an agent to take it through a
formula.

@enumerate
@item
Open the cockpit: @kbd{M-x gascity-dashboard}.  The @strong{Work}
section lists ready beads; put point on yours and press @key{RET} to
open it in beads.el (@pxref{Route Work}).
@item
From any gascity buffer, press @kbd{S} to raise the sling
(@pxref{Sling and Dispatch}).  @kbd{A} picks the work by completion (the
bead at point is offered first); @kbd{C-u} @kbd{A} takes free text
instead.
@item
@kbd{f} picks a formula, @kbd{T} picks the target agent, and the
generated infix keys fill the formula's variables (@pxref{Typed
variables}).  The live footer shows what will be dispatched
(@pxref{The live footer}).
@item
@kbd{P} opens the preview first (@pxref{The preview buffer}); @kbd{s}
launches.  The echo area confirms the dispatch and the new run appears
in the target rig.
@end enumerate

@noindent
Watch it from the Runs view: @kbd{j r} (or @kbd{M-x gascity-runs}),
then @key{RET} on the run for the step ladder, where each step's state
comes from its bead (@pxref{Runs and Live Events}).  When the run closes, its source
bead closes with it.

@node Recover a stalled agent
@section Recover a stalled agent

@cindex stalled agent, example
@cindex nudge, example
An agent stopped reporting progress.

@enumerate
@item
@kbd{j a} opens the Agents list.  Stalled agents sort to the top; the
@strong{Needs you} section of the cockpit also flags a run whose steps
have not moved for @code{gascity-dashboard-run-idle-threshold}
(@pxref{The Agents view}).
@item
@key{RET} on the agent attaches its terminal, @kbd{i} opens the polecat
detail with its work, run, transcript, mail and history
(@pxref{Agent Detail}).  @kbd{v} peeks at recent output read-only.
@item
@kbd{M} nudges the agent with a message (@pxref{Actions}).  If it is
suspended, @kbd{w} wakes it; if it is wedged, @kbd{R} resets the
session (confirming first), and @kbd{K} force-kills it if needed.
@item
@kbd{d} opens the agent's worktree in Dired so you can inspect what it
left behind.
@end enumerate

@noindent
None of these block: each reports in the echo area, and the row shows
@samp{@dots{}} until @command{gc} answers (@pxref{Actions}).

@node Open and drive a remote city
@section Open and drive a remote city

@cindex remote city, example
Open any directory of the city over TRAMP, then start a view from there
(@pxref{Remote Cities}):

@example
C-x d /ssh:user@@example.com:/home/user/city/ @key{RET}
M-x gascity-dashboard
@end example

@noindent
The header shows @samp{@@example.com}, every read and action runs
@command{gc} on that host, and @kbd{t} attaches the agent's tmux session
through a local @code{ssh -t} (@pxref{Remote buffers}).  @kbd{W} toggles
that city's live event stream, and a host that cannot be reached flips
the city to @samp{○ offline @@example.com} instead of erroring in every
section (@pxref{Scheduling}).

